From 17c7592946694c523a778376eb4b41104d179373 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] skippy: report native trace spans and GPU command timing

skippy/tracing.h reports where time goes inside a native call, for a
caller that already times the call itself. skippy_set_native_span_callback
delivers one span per finished phase of host work, on the thread that did
it: Skippy batch preparation, output handling and sampling; llama decode,
batch init, memory slot search, output reserve, per-ubatch graph build,
allocation, input upload and submission, output extraction, the recurrent
state sync and synchronize (the host waiting for the device); sampler
chains, each sampler and grammar; tokenization; KV and recurrent memory
operations, KV page export and import, and sequence state save and
restore; graph reserve, backend and memory creation, and model load
(GGUF open, hyperparameters, vocabulary, tensors and their data) at
startup. Spans nest under the open
span on their thread, else under the tag the caller set with
skippy_set_trace_tag, and work a thread does on another's behalf can adopt
that thread's open span as its parent; span ids carry
SKIPPY_NATIVE_SPAN_ID_BIT so they never collide with caller tags.
skippy_set_gpu_command_timing_callback reports, for each unit of device
work, when it was encoded and when the GPU ran it, carrying the submitting
thread's tag. SKIPPY_FEATURE_NATIVE_TRACING advertises the symbols; with no
callback installed a span costs one atomic load.

Backends expose the capability through three registry procs,
ggml_backend_set_command_timing_callback, ggml_backend_set_command_timing_tag
and ggml_backend_set_trace_span_callback, so Skippy never links a backend
directly. Metal times each command buffer with a completed handler
(GPUStartTime/GPUEndTime) and its encode window, numbering graph computes
like GGML_METAL_CAPTURE_COMPUTE so a slow graph seen in a trace can be
captured for Xcode by number. It reports compiling each kernel library,
opening the pipeline archive, initialising the device library and compiling
each pipeline on first use, allocating and mapping buffers and their
residency sets, blocking uploads and downloads, output copies, graph
optimisation and waiting on the last command buffer. Device initialisation runs before anyone can
listen, so spans that end before the first span callback are kept, a
bounded number, and delivered when it arrives. A dispatch worker encoding a
command buffer carries that buffer's tag, so a pipeline compiled there
lands under the call that submitted the graph.

Each command timing record also says what its graph nodes did. Backends
classify nodes as attention (flash attention, or the masked soft max and
K/Q, K/Q/V products of unfused attention), mat-muls with weights,
recurrent and linear-attention state (SSM, RWKV, gated linear attention,
delta-net updates and their cache copies), KV cache writes, and other
work; views and reshapes have no class. The record counts nodes and the
bytes they read per class (a gather or row write counts only its rows, not
the cache it indexes), the model layer of its first named node, the most
recurrent states one gather reads and how many of them are copied back
unchanged because no sequence of the batch owns them, and for attention
the most KV cells one query addresses (its row list for per-sequence row
lists, else the K/V span, or the K/Q product's K span for unfused
attention), the query rows, and how many attention nodes read row lists or
are flash attention.
With GGML_METAL_OP_PROFILE=1 and command timing installed, Metal splits
each graph into one command buffer per run of same-class nodes, encoded in
order on the calling thread, so the GPU times every class by itself;
splitting serialises the graph and stops fusion across class boundaries,
so totals grow while shares stay meaningful.
The first compute of a graph with a new node count reports an
mtl.graph_ops span whose detail counts the graph's nodes by op, so the
graphs two runtimes build for the same model can be compared op by op.

Bumps the Skippy ABI to 0.1.68.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
